---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const decayCfg = {
  curves: [
    { expr: '100*exp(-0.693*x/8)', label: 'I-131 (half-life 8 days)', color: '#7c3aed', width: 2.2 },
    { expr: '100*exp(-0.693*x/30)', label: 'Cs-137 (30 years, scaled)', color: '#b45309', width: 2.0 },
    { expr: '100*exp(-0.693*x/0.02)', label: 'a 72-second nuclide', color: '#0f766e', width: 1.6 },
  ],
  x: { min: 0, max: 40, label: 'time (units per nuclide)' },
  y: { min: 0, max: 105, label: '% activity remaining' },
  crossings: false,
  height: 340,
};

const radiationCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Unstable nuclei decay', note: 'Too many protons or neutrons, and the nucleus emits: alpha (a helium nucleus), beta (an electron), or gamma (pure energy). Each has different penetration.' },
    { math: '2 · Penetration differs wildly', note: 'Alpha stops in paper (dangerous only inside the body); beta stops in plastic or a few mm of tissue; gamma needs centimetres of lead.' },
    { math: '3 · Exponential decay rules time', note: 'Activity halves every half-life — never truly zero. Decay heat and waste timelines are this curve, multiplied by thousands of nuclides.' },
    { math: '4 · Detection counts interactions', note: 'Geiger tubes count ion pairs; scintillators count light flashes; semiconductors count energy deposits. Detection is statistics, not certainty.' },
    { math: '5 · Dose, not just presence', note: 'Hazard depends on activity, energy, and where the source sits (inside vs outside). Protection = time, distance, shielding — always in that order.' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/nuclear-engineering/foundational/radiation-physics-and-detection">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Nuclear engineering is built on the physics of unstable nuclei: what decays, what it emits,
    how those emissions interact with matter, and how they are detected. Every later topic —
    reactor criticality, shielding design, waste timelines — is applied radiation physics, plus
    one relentless curve: exponential decay.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="decay, penetration, detection — and dose" widget="equationStep" config={radiationCfg} />

  <h2>Half-lives set the rules of the game</h2>
  <p>
    Exponential decay means every radioisotope has a character: iodine-131 (8 days) is intense and
    brief — perfect for medical therapy, dangerous for weeks; caesium-137 (30 years) defines the
    long shadow of accidents; and a 72-second nuclide is gone before you measure it twice. Compare
    the curves: the physics of waste policy is visible in three exponentials.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="three half-lives, three timelines" widget="plotter" config={decayCfg} />

  <h2>Detection is counting, carefully</h2>
  <p>
    Radiation detectors convert single interactions into signals: gas-filled Geiger tubes click,
    scintillators flash, semiconductors resolve energies. Because emission is random, detection is
    statistical — a count rate carries uncertainty, and competent measurement includes the error
    bars. Nuclear engineering's first lab lesson is that you can never observe a single atom's
    schedule, only the ensemble's law.
  </p>

  <h2>Protection: time, distance, shielding</h2>
  <p>
    Dose falls with exposure time, with the square of distance, and with shielding matched to the
    radiation type (lead for gamma, plastic then lead for beta, nothing helps with alpha outside
    the body). Add ALARA — every exposure justified and minimised — and you have the operating
    culture of the entire nuclear industry, from reactors to radiology.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> ABET nuclear program criteria; Krane, <em>Introductory Nuclear Physics</em>; Knoll, <em>Radiation Detection and Measurement</em>.</p>
</TopicPage>
